// @check
// @error if-condition must be boolean, got i64 | boolean
// THE JOIN IS SOUND, NOT GENEROUS (must-error). Only one arm writes here, so on the path where
// `c` is false `x` is still the `i64` the guard proved — and the join of the two paths is
// therefore `i64 | boolean`, which `if x` cannot read. The refusal is the join's own answer,
// not the absence of one.
//
// This is the witness [D1846](../../../docs/internals/inventory/D1846.md) was first filed on,
// and re-filing the row was part of its close: the program is refused with the join and without
// it, so it could never have shown the gap. `narrow-arm-join.vl` carries the shapes the join
// does decide — both arms writing the same member, an arm that diverges, and a three-member
// union whose join is a proper subset of the declaration.
//
// Hoisting the read into the arm that wrote is the fix, and it compiles.
function f(x: i64 | boolean, c: boolean) {
  if x is i64 {
    if c { x = true }
    if x { print(1) } else { print(2) }
  }
}
f(3, true)
